let's talk about what's been happening  in Chile this is a country some people  have described as an economic success  story  but we're also seeing this protesters  angry at not being able to put food on  the table get proper health care and  just generally with how the countries  run so what are they seeing that others  aren't  is there a flipside to chill a success  story whether Chile can be described as  successful depends on who you ask and  ultimately where their politics lie  let's start with a look at Chile's  economy and really any story about the  country's modern economy should start  with these guys the Chicago boys they're  a famous some would say infamous group  of chilean neoliberal economists in the  1970s they studied in Chicago under  Milton Friedman a champion of  free-market capitalism and an eventual  Nobel laureate it was around that time  back in Chile that General Augusto  Pinochet staged a military coup to get  rid of President Salvador Allende and  his socialist government Chile's economy  was a mess yearly inflation was running  around 300 percent and a lot of people  struggled to pay for basic necessities  so Pinochet needed a plan to fix the  economy and the Chicago boys had won it  went like this the best social public  policy is economic growth if the economy  grows you will have less poverty and so  on along the time in thus far Chicago  voice the recommendation was right  Chile's economy did grow thanks to  freeing up the market Chile made trade  deals with countries around the world  exporting things like copper and it's  internationally renowned wines in 2010  Chile even became the first Latin  American country to join the OECD a  Clark for developed economies applause  back-slapping and especially  congratulations were an order at Chile's  presidential palace Chile's economy has  had its bumps but compared to its Latin  American neighbors the country has  become a sort of promised land between  2017 and 2018 Chile's economy grew by  about twenty billion dollars compare  that to Argentina where the economy sure  by a hundred and twenty three billion  dollars in that time  it's no wonder tens of thousands of  migrants from all over South America  have moved to Chile for jobs in the last  few years the leading countries in GDP  per capita we have reduced our poverty  from nearly 40 percent in 1990 to down  28.6% last year so what that rosy  picture in mind it's tough to imagine  why a four cent increase in metro ticket  prices led to the biggest protest in  Chile in years the only people kicked it  off by refusing to pay for the Metro  soon the protests grew and spread the  unrest on the streets of the Chilean  capital and elsewhere in the country has  been unrelenting but it also got ugly  people started burning stuff there was  looting and the government deployed the  military protesters and police  confronted each other thousands were  injured on both sides and people had  died  now this movement has been about a lot  of things but one of the big ones is  inequality 33% of Chilean GDP goes to  the one-percenters that's higher than  almost everywhere I don't think there's  another country has that level of  inequality  well many protesters say is that the  middle class in Chile isn't middle class  at all that's because in the government  size a person can qualify as middle  class even if they earn as little as a  hundred and eighty dollars a month for a  family of four it's about six hundred  and thirty dollars I would be  classifying  amongst the 20 richest percent in the  country and Yee monthly income is about  nine nine fifty euros a month and I'm  rational with a master's degree I don't  think that's a rich person Chile's  National Statistics Institute calculated  that eighty percent of people have no  money left at the end of the month once  they get through paying for food  transport housing and basic services  it's all gone unless they pay for stuff  and installments which in Chile you can  do just about anywhere a lot of people  go to the supermarket with a credit card  they don't even have enough to pay for  that supermarket bill in cash so in a  way we have seen a decrease in poverty  but we have more people in debt the  protesters have another criticism and  that's against high levels of  privatization in Chile utilities like  water and electricity are in the hands  of private companies whereas sectors  like education and health have a mix of  private and public options critics  though say that system is driving prices  up and that the quality of public  offerings are worsening now if a retiree  hasn't paid into a private pension for  instance they get a government allowance  of  one hundred and eighty dollars of them  we have people who are receiving  pensions after a word from their whole  lives which are making them poor they  don't cover their basic needs with a  pension that they are getting access to  good health care that is really  expensive access to private schools  that's really expensive access to some  universities that's really expensive now  in response the Chilean government has  raised the minimum wage promised more  money for health services and the very  elderly but some protesters say won't be  enough and now there's division over  just how much Chile's free-market model  should change there are two perspective  for the crisis in Chile one is Chile's  poor country unequal Society also we  need to change everything the another is  much more social democracy pension funds  the health and so on but we need to keep  the competitiveness of the economy and  that's the government's message yes we  recognize the need for social fairness  but we cannot change things overnight  and also we need to keep a country that  keeps an economy working because if the  discussion is about redistributing  wealth well we first need to create that  world moving ahead won't be easy there's  been damage Chile's National Human  Rights Institute accuses the police and  military of serious abuses that include  using excessive force during the  demonstrations even Chile's famously  stable economy had its biggest drop in a  decade and it's probably not going to  recover for a while but there is a plan  to reconcile the country it involves  rewriting the Constitution that was  originally drafted during Pinochet  dictatorship polls in Chile say it's  something 80% of Chileans seem to  support and so does the president yo  creo que aqui es a sublime a importancia  recuperar el valor de la unidad el  respeto  del V a logo de los acuerdo z-- y de la  buena voluntad con que debemos real  hacen a knows enter nosotros y special  go look appears anything in April a  referendum will ask voters whether they  want a new constitution and whether it  should be written by an alt citizen body  or include lawmakers - if it goes ahead  elections for that body are expected to  happen in October it's gonna be a long  and politically difficult process but  the idea is that Chile can write a news  story if you will a story more people  can call a success if there's a story in  the news or if there's a topic you want  to get your head around get in touch  with us on Twitter Facebook or YouTube  and make sure to subscribe to Algiers  channels and pages so you can't miss our  next episode

